post_install() {
  cat <<'MSG'

steel-backup: not yet configured, and deliberately not started.

  Set it up as each user, not as root — a locked home is an opaque
  encrypted image, so backups run from inside the session:

      steel-backup setup
      steel-backup probe      # proves the credential cannot delete history
      steel-backup run
      steel-backup verify     # restores a sample and compares hashes
      systemctl --user enable --now steel-backup.timer steel-backup-verify.timer

  Three things this refuses to do, and why:

    * A target on this disk. A backup destroyed by the same wipe that
      destroys the volume is not a backup.

    * A remote target whose credential can delete. Without append-only, an
      adversary with the unlocked machine deletes the backups first and then
      the local wipe is total.

    * Keeping the outer PRIVATE key here. The outer layer exists so a seized
      machine cannot decrypt its own history; the private half belongs on a
      token, on paper, or with someone you trust.

  If you have decided not to back up, record it — `steel-backup disable` —
  so that steel-check reports a decision rather than a broken measure. Note
  that with no off-device backup, duress key destruction is permanent.

MSG
}
